# Erdős Problem #99 — sharp tangent capacity and exact three-bar flattening

**Date:** 2026-09-21–22  
**Starting commit:** `84d937ca93decb710b829fc610d46e0d89684988`  
**State version:** `2026-09-14.audit.51`  
**Route:** Target B, pure-disk higher-order train obstruction  
**Claim classification:** proved candidates awaiting promotion; the generated
artifact lifecycle remains unreviewed. No independent promotion audit is claimed.  
**Active prompt:** Target B route map, read after the authority/dependency bootstrap.  
**Literature imports:** none.  
**Level I / Target B status:** unchanged, open.

## 1. Exact new statements and their scope

The [Section-120 companion](../../research/target_b_section120_nonpositive_capacity.md)
explicitly leaves open a universal factor-two capacity bound for linear spaces of
tangent quartic blockers. Its proved factor two covers only the paired construction
in canonical Target B §43.1. This report closes that algebraic gap and then derives
a finite diameter-descent criterion. Sections 5.2–5.3 then use curved train paths
and actual source gradients to remove the entire two-persistent-bar higher-order
blocking case, going beyond the initial abstract cubic analysis.
Sections 5.4–5.6 extend this to any number of affinely independent rotation
levels, then eliminate the entire fully supported **three**-persistent-bar
higher-order case by an exact phase-group gradient balance.

The implications are:

```text
cubic polarization
  -> rank of an actual relative-tangent map <= floor(tangent support / 2)
  -> per-bar and joint quotient capacity, with exact boundary-detour payment;

two persistent equality bars with nonzero cubic moments
  -> joint relative-tangent rank <= half their total active support;
violation of this bound, or coprime nonzero cubic moments
  -> a common strict cubic descent direction
  -> an actual same-cardinality, unit-separated configuration of diameter < D;

actual source gradients + second-order train correction
  -> two persistent bars have opposing nonzero gradients
  -> both are exactly constant on every backward-kernel train line
  -> a diameter-pair-minimal source has only those two active bars;

three persistent bars, with a positive balance on all three
  -> strict stress-weight transfer + phase-group gradient balance
  -> all three bars are exactly constant on the backward-kernel slice.
```

The initial joint bound includes correlated two-bar obstructions, not only coprime
ones; the later source-specific theorem eliminates that higher-order obstruction
altogether when there are only two persistent bars and `D>1`.
The first supplies the previously missing universal *linear-space*
capacity; it does not bound the dimension of an arbitrary nonlinear cubic zero
set. Neither result removes the full backward-rank case, general correlated
multi-bar cubic obstructions, or exceptional-face topology.

Only canonical [Target B §§37,40–43](../targets/target-b/01-jammed-rhombic-branch.md)
and [shared Theorem 10.6](../theory/01-local-structure.md) are needed for the
geometric applications. The Section-120 boundary charge is reproduced below,
so its audited-working lifecycle is not silently upgraded. No Target C
stress-rigidity premise is used. The apparently useful ZRF/pure-disk composition
was checked and is already subsumed by the September 11 pure-rhombic exclusion;
it is not a new claim here.

## 2. Cubic rank and the even-support equality theorem

**Theorem TC.** Let `W` be a real linear subspace of `R^m`, and let every
`c_i` be a nonzero real number. If

\[
\sum_{i=1}^m c_i w_i^3=0\qquad(w\in W),
\]

and exactly `s` coordinate functionals are nonzero on `W`, then

\[
\boxed{\dim W\le\lfloor s/2\rfloor.}
\tag{TC1}
\]

If `s=2d` and `dim W=d`, put `z_i=c_i^{1/3}w_i` using real cube roots
and discard the identically zero coordinates. After a coordinate permutation,

\[
\boxed{W_z=\{(x_1,-x_1,\ldots,x_d,-x_d):x\in\mathbb R^d\}.}
\tag{TC2}
\]

**Proof.** Diagonal rescaling reduces to `c_i=1`. Delete inactive coordinates.
Choose `a in W` outside the finite union of the coordinate kernels; all `a_i`
are nonzero. Polarizing the cubic identity gives

\[
\sum_i a_i u_i v_i=0\qquad(u,v\in W).
\tag{TC3}
\]

The nondegenerate diagonal bilinear form `diag(a)` has signature `(p,q)`,
where `p+q=s`. A totally isotropic subspace has dimension at most `min(p,q)`:
its projection to either definite coordinate subspace is injective. This proves
TC1, including spaces with inactive coordinates.

For equality `s=2d`, both signatures must be `d`. Split coordinates according
to the sign of `a`. Both coordinate projections are isomorphisms on `W`,
so `W={(x,Ax):x in R^d}` for an invertible real matrix `A`. Its polynomial
identity is

\[
\sum_i x_i^3+\sum_j(Ax)_j^3=0.
\]

Taking Hessians and then determinants yields

\[
\prod_i x_i=(-1)^d\det(A)^2\prod_j(Ax)_j.
\tag{TC4}
\]

Unique factorization into real linear forms forces the rows of `A` to be
nonzero multiples of distinct coordinate functionals. Substitution into the
cubic identity forces each multiplier to have cube `-1`, hence to equal `-1`.
This proves TC2, also when `d=1`; `d=0` is vacuous. No generic-position or
generic-rank hypothesis about the geometric source was used. ∎

## 3. The actual tangent map, rather than ambient motion dimension

Fix an active diameter bar `h`, of length `D`, in a finite pure strict-unit-rhombus
disk. Let `X` be a real linear space of train velocities such that the bar is
first-order neutral and every non-tangent separating train rotates at the common
bar velocity `s_h(v)`. These are precisely the §43 equality hypotheses. For the
`m_h` tangent separating trains define the linear map

\[
A_h:X\longrightarrow\mathbb R^{m_h},\qquad
(A_hv)_\tau=v_\tau-s_h(v).
\tag{TC5}
\]

Write their oriented contributions as `sigma_tau J e_h`, where
`sigma_tau in {+1,-1}` and `e_h=r_h/D`. No sign-coherence assertion is needed.
For `w=A_hv` put `S_j(w)=sum sigma_tau w_tau^j`. First-order neutrality is
`S_1=0`. In a frame rotating at `s_h(v)`, the exact bar vector is

\[
r_h(t)=\left(D-\sum_\tau\sigma_\tau\sin(tw_\tau)\right)e_h
 +\sum_\tau\sigma_\tau[\cos(tw_\tau)-1]Je_h.
\tag{TC6}
\]

Thus

\[
|r_h(t)|^2-D^2={D\over3}S_3(w)t^3+{S_2(w)^2\over4}t^4+O(t^5).
\tag{TC7}
\]

**Corollary TB.** If this individual bar is nondecreasing for both signs of
sufficiently small `t` along every line with velocity in `X`, then `S_3`
vanishes identically on `A_hX`. Consequently, with `s_h^{act}` the number of
nonzero coordinate functionals of `A_h`,

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}A_h\le\lfloor s_h^{act}/2\rfloor
\le\lfloor m_h/2\rfloor.}
\tag{TC8}
\]

The same conclusion holds if only cubic vanishing on `X` is assumed. It is
the rank of the *relative tangent image* that is bounded. Directions in
`ker A_h`, including motions of trains invisible to this bar, must not be
counted as blocked directions.

For `m_h=2d` and rank `d`, TC2 says the signed deviations
`z_tau=sigma_tau w_tau` pair exactly as `x_j,-x_j`. All odd signed moments
then vanish, and TC6 becomes the exact identity

\[
|r_h(t)|^2-D^2=
\left[\sum_{j=1}^d(\sigma_{i_j}+\sigma_{k_j})
                  (\cos(tx_j)-1)\right]^2.
\tag{TC9}
\]

If the fourth-order coefficient is required to be strictly positive for every
nonzero image vector, its quadratic moment is definite. In the equality case
TC9 this happens exactly when all pairs have the same sign and both members
of each pair have that sign. Thus a maximal uniformly quartic family on even
support forces a common orientation on its active tangent coordinates. This
is a *conclusion at equality*, not a geometric assumption for arbitrary trains.

For common orientation, cubic vanishing is also sufficient for a uniform local
quartic barrier. If `rho=||w||_2` and
`|t|rho<=min(1,1/D)`, Taylor's inequalities give

\[
\left|\sum\sin(tw_\tau)\right|\le {|t|^5\rho^5\over120},
\qquad
\sum(1-\cos(tw_\tau))\ge {11\over24}t^2\rho^2.
\]

Inserting these in TC6 proves the explicit finite bound

\[
\boxed{|r_h(t)|^2-D^2\ge {1\over8}t^4\rho^4.}
\tag{TC10}
\]

Indeed `121/576-1/60=557/2880>1/8`. This estimate is uniform over the image
unit sphere; no direction-dependent remainder is being used as a uniform one.

## 4. Boundary payment and simultaneous quotient capacity

Let `Gamma` be either outer-boundary arc between the endpoints of `h`, with
unit-edge length `L_h`. Every separating train charges exactly one terminal
edge occurrence on this arc, with its oriented displacement `a_tau`. Distinct
trains charge distinct occurrences. With `q=r_h/D`,

\[
L_h-D=\sum_{e\in\Gamma}(1-q\cdot e).
\]

All summands are nonnegative; each tangent terminal contributes one. Hence
`m_h<=L_h-D`, and TC8 gives the universal linear-space replacement for the
paired-only Section-120 statement:

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}A_h\le
 \left\lfloor{m_h\over2}\right\rfloor
 \le\left\lfloor{L_h-D\over2}\right\rfloor.}
\tag{TC11}
\]

On the shorter arc, `L_h<=T`, so the last bound is `floor((T-D)/2)`.
The proof uses proper train crosscuts of a disk. It does not pass through
holes or exceptional faces without an additional theorem.

For a family `J` of equality bars whose cubics all vanish on one common
linear space `X`, rank subadditivity gives

\[
\boxed{\dim X-\dim\bigcap_{h\in J}\ker A_h
 \le\sum_{h\in J}\left\lfloor{s_h^{act}\over2}\right\rfloor.}
\tag{TC12}
\]

If `J` contains all active diameter bars, a velocity in the intersection
rotates every separating contribution at its own bar's common speed.
Therefore TC6 preserves every active diameter **exactly** along the train-angle
line. All contact lengths also stay exactly one; all inactive pair inequalities
retain their finite strict slack for sufficiently small time. After quotienting
common rotation, a nonzero such velocity gives a noncongruent same-diameter
family. This is not a strict diameter improvement. TC12 controls a common
quotient; it does not replace the joint map by a sum of independent capacities.

## 5. Cubic descent eliminates a further actual source case

Use canonical §42 notation, in the rotation quotient:

\[
K=\ker\mathcal B\subseteq\mathcal L.
\]

Every active bar's quadratic form is nonpositive on `K`. Define its
**persistent equality set**

\[
J=\{h:H_h|_K\equiv0\}.
\]

For each `h in J`, the positive terms of the signed-variance formula vanish
on all of `K`, while negative incidences already vanish by `K=ker B`.
Thus TC5–TC7 apply throughout `K`. Write the homogeneous cubic
`F_h(v)=S_3(A_hv)`.

**Theorem CD.** If there is `v in K` with `F_h(v)<0` for every `h in J`,
then the source admits a same-cardinality feasible configuration with diameter
strictly below `D`.

**Proof.** The strict cubic inequalities define an open subset of `K`. For each
`h notin J`, the zero set of its nonpositive quadratic form is a proper linear
subspace. Avoid their finite union inside that open subset. At the resulting
velocity, every bar outside `J` decreases at quadratic order and every bar in
`J` decreases at cubic order for small positive time. The finite set of bars
therefore all decrease simultaneously along the exact train-angle path.
Every unit contact is preserved. Every other lower and upper pair inequality
has positive initial slack and remains feasible for sufficiently small time.
The maximum of the finitely many pair distances is then strictly below `D`. ∎

In a local diameter minimum with `K!=0`, the set `J` is nonempty by the same
finite-union argument at quadratic order. If `J={h}`, CD forces `F_h=0`
identically (a nonzero odd cubic takes a negative value), hence TC8 applies
without an additional cubic-flat hypothesis.

**Two-bar consequence.** If `J={h_1,h_2}` and both cubics are nonzero and
relatively prime over the real polynomial ring on `K`, CD supplies a strict
diameter improvement. Thus a two-bar survivor with nonzero cubics must share
a real odd-degree factor.

Here is the algebraic proof, including the real-root issue. If no point makes
both cubics negative, oddness also forbids both being positive. Hence
`F_1 F_2<=0` everywhere. Factor `F_1` over the reals. Since its degree is
odd, it has an irreducible odd-degree factor `f` of odd multiplicity. Such
a factor has a smooth real zero where it changes sign: choose a direction in
which its leading homogeneous part is nonzero, and a generic parallel line.
The resulting univariate polynomial has odd degree and, by squarefreeness in
characteristic zero, simple roots for generic lines. It has a real simple root.
If `f` divided neither the other factors nor `F_2`, their nonzero resultants
allow the same generic line to avoid all their zeros at roots of `f`. Near
that real root, the product would change sign, a contradiction. Therefore
`f` divides `F_2` as well. The possible shared odd degrees for cubics are one
and three. In particular, if `F_1` is irreducible, then necessarily
`F_2=-c F_1` for some `c>0`.

This is an exact necessary algebraic correlation in the *actual* source's
neutral space. Abstract disjoint train supports do not establish coprimality
after restriction to `K`; the source-specific restriction must be computed or
proved. If one cubic is identically zero, this two-nonzero-cubic consequence
does not apply.

### 5.1. A joint capacity bound for two correlated bars

**Theorem JC.** Suppose the source is a local diameter minimum, `K!=0`,
`J={h_1,h_2}`, and both restricted cubic moments `F_i` are nonzero. Let `m_i`
be the number of nonzero coordinate functionals of `A_i:K -> R^{m_i}` after
discarding inactive tangent coordinates. Then

\[
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}(A_1,A_2)
 \le \left\lfloor{m_1+m_2\over2}\right\rfloor.}
\tag{JC1}
\]

The right side improves by one if both `m_i` are even. It also improves by
one when `m_1+m_2` is even and the cubics are proportional. Thus even-total
saturation requires both supports odd and the cubics nonproportional.
Violation of these bounds implies an actual finite strict diameter decrease,
by CD. This is a joint quotient rank, not a bound on all of `K`.

**Proof.** CD and oddness give `F_1 F_2<=0` on `K`. Two nonzero real homogeneous
cubics with this property have one of the following forms:

1. `F_2=-c F_1` for a constant `c>0`; or
2. `F_1=ell Q_1`, `F_2=-ell Q_2`, where `ell` is a nonzero linear form and
   both `Q_i` are nonzero positive-semidefinite quadratic forms.

To justify this classification, the odd-factor argument above gives a common
real factor of degree one or three. A degree-three irreducible factor forces
case 1. Otherwise divide out a common linear factor `ell`, obtaining quadratics
whose product is everywhere nonpositive (by continuity also on `ell=0`).
If either quadratic is indefinite, it changes sign at every smooth point of
its real null cone, forcing the other to vanish on that cone. An indefinite
quadratic determines its quadratic vanishing ideal in degree two: after
Sylvester diagonalization, evaluations on positive/negative coordinate pairs,
then on two coordinates of the same sign paired with one of the opposite sign,
force proportional coefficients and zero cross terms; adjoining radical
coordinates forces their coefficients to vanish too. Equivalently, in rank two
its two distinct real linear factors must both divide the other quadratic.
Thus the quadratics are negative scalar multiples, giving case 1. If neither
is indefinite, they have opposite signs and may be written as in case 2 after
possibly replacing `ell` by `-ell`.

In case 1, concatenate the two tangent images. The polynomial
`F_1+c^{-1}F_2` is a diagonal cubic with every coefficient nonzero and vanishes
on the joint image. TC proves JC1. If the total support is even and equality
holds, TC2 pairs the cube-root-rescaled coordinates. Within each bar the
rescaling has the form `z_i=beta_h sigma_i w_i` with `beta_h>0`; hence its
first-moment identity is `sum z_i=0`. An independent pair `(x_j,-x_j)` cannot
straddle the two bars, since it would contribute a nonzero coefficient of
`x_j` to each bar's first moment. Every pair therefore lies within one bar.
Each individual cubic is then identically zero, a contradiction. This gives
the improvement by one in the proportional even-total case.

In case 2, `ell` factors through each `A_i`. Indeed, if `k in ker A_i` but
`ell(k)!=0`, translating a vector with `F_i(v)!=0` by a multiple of `k` to
the hyperplane `ell=0` would preserve `F_i` but also make it zero. Thus `ell`
is in both row spaces. Restriction to `ker ell` makes both cubics identically
zero. TC therefore yields

\[
\operatorname{rank}A_i\le \lfloor m_i/2\rfloor+1.
\tag{JC2}
\]

For **even** `m_i=2d`, this improves to `rank A_i<=d`. Suppose instead that
`rank A_i=d+1`. The image of `ker ell` has dimension `d`; TC forces its full
`2d`-coordinate support, and TC2 writes its signed coordinates as
`(x_1,-x_1,...,x_d,-x_d)`. Choose one extra image vector
`a=(a_1,b_1,...,a_d,b_d)`. Its first moment gives
`sum_j(a_j+b_j)=0`. On this `(d+1)`-dimensional image, the cubic is

\[
\begin{aligned}
F(x,t)={}&3t\sum_j(a_j+b_j)x_j^2
 +3t^2\sum_j(a_j^2-b_j^2)x_j
 +t^3\sum_j(a_j^3+b_j^3).
\end{aligned}
\tag{JC3}
\]

The induced `ell` is a nonzero multiple of `t`. The quotient `F/ell` is
semidefinite: pull it back by a linear right inverse of `A_i` to use the
semidefinite cofactor on `K`. Consequently its restriction at `t=0` is
semidefinite. All the numbers `a_j+b_j` must have the same weak sign, while
their sum is zero. They all vanish. But then `a` was already in the paired
wall, a contradiction.

We have proved `rank A_i<=m_i/2` for even `m_i`, and
`rank A_i<=(m_i+1)/2` for odd `m_i`. Since both row spaces contain the nonzero
form `ell`,

\[
\operatorname{rank}(A_1,A_2)
 \le\operatorname{rank}A_1+\operatorname{rank}A_2-1.
\tag{JC4}
\]

Combining the parity cases proves JC1, with a one-unit improvement when both
supports are even. Together with case 1, this proves all the statements. ∎

In particular, using the disk boundary charge `m_i<=L_i-D`, a source violating
`rank(A_1,A_2)<=floor((L_1+L_2-2D)/2)` cannot be locally minimizing under the
stated two-bar hypotheses. This conclusion uses the actual restricted maps;
two individually large ranks do not imply a large joint rank.

### 5.2. Curved train paths and the persistent-gradient stress cone

In this subsection and the next, write `g_h` for the full train-angle gradient
of `f_h=|r_h|^2-D^2` at the source, **twice** the gradient in canonical §41.
Write `Hhat_h=2H_h` for its full Hessian. These factors do not change any
kernel, sign, or normalized gradient balance. The gradient annihilates common
rotation, so balancing gradients in the rotation
quotient is equivalent to balancing their full coordinate vectors. Define

\[
\mathcal P=\{\mu\ge0:\ \sum_{h\in J}\mu_h=1,
                  \ \sum_{h\in J}\mu_h g_h=0\}.
\tag{CG1}
\]

**Theorem CG.** For a local diameter minimum with `K!=0`, `P` is nonempty
(the generic-velocity specialization of canonical §42.4).
Let its finitely many vertices be `mu^(1),...,mu^(q)`. If there is `v in K`
such that

\[
\sum_{h\in J}\mu_h^{(j)}F_h(v)<0\qquad(1\le j\le q),
\tag{CG2}
\]

then there is an actual finite strict diameter descent. Consequently, if `P`
is a singleton `{mu}`, the weighted cubic vanishes identically on `K`:

\[
\boxed{\sum_{h\in J}\mu_h F_h\equiv0.}
\tag{CG3}
\]

**Proof.** Choose `v in K` avoiding the proper quadratic kernels of all bars
outside `J`. If `P` is empty, the strict linear alternative supplies `a` with
`g_h a<0` for every `h in J`. The exact train path
`theta(t)=theta(0)+tv+epsilon t^2 a` decreases these bars at second order.
Choose fixed `epsilon>0` small enough that the strictly negative quadratic
coefficients of bars outside `J` remain negative. This is a finite descent.

For CG2 choose `v` in its open set, again avoiding those quadratic kernels.
The strict linear alternative now supplies `b` such that

\[
g_h b+{D\over3}F_h(v)<0\qquad(h\in J).
\]

Indeed the obstructions are exactly the nonnegative gradient balances, and
checking their normalized polytope is equivalent to checking all its vertices.
Along `theta(t)=theta(0)+tv+t^3 b`, every persistent bar decreases at cubic
order, while every other active bar still decreases at quadratic order.
Contacts remain exactly unit and inactive inequalities retain their slack.
If `P` is a singleton, any nonzero weighted cubic takes a negative value by
oddness, contradicting local optimality. This proves CG3. ∎

For a singleton `P` positive on a set `I`, TC applies to the joint image of
the maps `A_h`, `h in I`, using coefficients `mu_h sigma_tau`. If that support
has even total size and at least one individual cubic is nonzero, its capacity
improves from half the total support to half minus one: the first-moment argument
in the proportional case of JC applies to any number of labeled bars. Thus CG3
supplies a source-specific cubic balance beyond the two-bar setting. Uniqueness
of `P` is a hypothesis, not a consequence of arbitrary local optimality.

### 5.3. Two persistent bars cannot supply any higher-order blocker

**Theorem PF.** In the same pure-disk setting, suppose `D>1`, the source is a
local diameter minimum, and `K!=0`. Then `|J|>=2`. If `J={h_1,h_2}`, both
gradients are nonzero and are opposite positive multiples. Moreover, along
**every** straight train-angle path with velocity `v in K`, both persistent
diameter lengths remain **exactly** `D` for all sufficiently small times.
In particular their tangent cubics vanish, and neither a cubic cusp nor a
quartic tangent obstruction can persist in this two-bar case.

There is a nonzero velocity in `K` along which every other active diameter
bar strictly decreases for sufficiently small nonzero time. Thus, if the source
is also diameter-pair-minimal among triangle-free configurations of that same
cardinality and diameter, then `J` is the whole active diameter set. Such a
source with at least three active diameter pairs must have `|J|>=3`.

**Proof: zero-gradient cases.** In full train coordinates,

\[
(g_h)_\tau=2r_h\cdot J(d_{h\tau}u_\tau).
\tag{PF1}
\]

If `g_h=0`, every separating contribution is parallel to `r_h`. Write them
as `+e_h` or `-e_h`, with respective counts `P_h,N_h`. Then
`D=P_h-N_h>1`, so `P_h>=2`. Rotating two positive contributions at speeds
`+1,-1`, leaving all others fixed, gives a direction `a` with
`Hhat_h[a,a]=-4D<0`. This is a genuine negative quadratic direction.

For `h in J` with zero gradient, its separating contributions all rotate at
one common speed along any `v in K`, by the persistent equality conditions.
Consequently along `theta(0)+tv+epsilon t^2 a`, its squared length change
is `epsilon^2 t^4 Hhat_h[a,a]/2+O(t^6)`: the common rotation factors out exactly.

If `J` consists of this one bar, use the negative direction just found. If
`J` has a second bar with nonzero gradient, choose `a` in the open negative
quadratic cone of the first with `g_2 a<0`. Such a choice exists because a
nonzero linear functional cannot vanish on an open set, and changing `a` to
`-a` preserves its negative quadratic value. If both gradients are zero,
their separating train sets are disjoint: a shared train would be parallel
to both diameter directions, which is impossible for distinct diameter bars.
Choose negative quadratic directions independently on the two disjoint sets.
In every case choose generic `v in K` and then sufficiently small `epsilon`
to retain negative quadratic coefficients on all bars outside `J`. This gives
a finite strict descent, contradicting local optimality.

Here we used the elementary fact that two distinct diameter bars cannot be
parallel. Orient parallel bars to have the same displacement `r`, of length
`D`, and let `w!=0` be the displacement between their starting endpoints.
The two cross distances have vectors `r+w` and `r-w`; at least one squared
length is at least `D^2+|w|^2>D^2`, a contradiction.

Thus for `|J|<=2` all gradients in `J` must be nonzero. Theorem CG rules out
`|J|=1`, and when `|J|=2` forces `g_1=-c g_2` with `c>0`.

**Proof: exact flattening.** We now specialize the established tangent transfer
in canonical §42.5, and control every equality bar rather than only a selected
stress support. Consider a train tangent to `h_1`. Its component
in `g_1` is nonzero, so its component in `g_2` is also nonzero. It therefore
separates `h_2`. It is not tangent to `h_2`, since distinct diameter directions
are not parallel. Persistent equality for `h_2` forces this train's velocity
to be `s_2(v)`. Every tangent train of `h_1` therefore has the same relative
velocity `delta=s_2(v)-s_1(v)`. First-order neutrality of `h_1` gives

\[
\delta\sum_{\tau\text{ tangent to }h_1}\sigma_\tau=0.
\tag{PF2}
\]

In TC6 the sine sum is now `sin(t delta) sum sigma_tau` and the cosine
increment is `(cos(t delta)-1) sum sigma_tau`. Both vanish identically: either
`delta=0` or the signed sum is zero. Hence `h_1` keeps its length exactly.
Interchanging the two bars gives the same conclusion for `h_2`. No sign
coherence or finite-order truncation is used.

Choose `v!=0` outside the quadratic kernels for bars outside `J`. These bars
strictly decrease, while the two persistent ones stay exactly at `D`. Contacts
stay exactly unit, and all noncontacts stay strictly above one for small time;
thus a triangle-free source remains triangle-free. No new diameter pair appears
because every initially inactive upper inequality has strict slack. If any bar
outside `J` existed, this would contradict diameter-pair minimality. ∎

This is an exclusion of the *two-persistent-bar higher-order obstruction*, not
an exclusion of all configurations with only two active diameter pairs. A
same-diameter family is not a strict improvement. The full backward-rank case,
three or more persistent bars, and non-disk topology remain outside PF.

### 5.4. More bars: a bounded integer relation is necessary for nonflatness

**Theorem AP.** Suppose a gradient balance in `P` is strictly positive on
**every** persistent bar. Group the rotation functionals `s_h in K*` into
distinct classes `s_1,...,s_m`. If these `m` functionals are affinely independent
(the differences from any one have rank `m-1`), every persistent bar is exactly
constant along every backward-kernel train line. In particular this holds when
there are at most two distinct rotation functionals, regardless of the number
of persistent bars.

More precisely, any persistent bar that is not identically constant on a
neighborhood of zero in the train-angle slice `theta(0)+K` supplies an integer
vector `c!=0` satisfying

\[
\boxed{\sum_{j=1}^m c_j=0,\qquad
 \sum_{j=1}^m c_j s_j=0,\qquad
 \|c\|_1\le2m_h\le2(L_h-D).}
\tag{AP1}
\]

The last inequality uses the disk boundary charge from §4. Thus even a real
affine dependence is insufficient without a bounded **integer** dependence.
This is a source-specific finite obstruction, not a generic-rank assertion.

**Proof.** Fix a tangent train of a persistent bar `h`. The positive gradient
balance forces another persistent bar `k` with a nonzero component on that
train. Nonparallel diameter directions make the train nontangent to `k`, and
persistent equality gives `v_tau=s_k(v)` on all of `K`. If more than one bar
can be chosen, all their functionals coincide on `K`, since they equal the
same coordinate functional `v_tau`. Thus the destination rotation class of
each tangent train is well-defined, independent of `v`.

Suppose `h` belongs to class `i`. Let `N_hj` be the signed number of its
tangent trains assigned to class `j`. First-order neutrality is the identity

\[
\sum_{j\ne i}N_{hj}(s_j-s_i)=0\quad\text{on }K.
\tag{AP2}
\]

If all these off-diagonal signed counts vanish, each destination class cancels
its sine sum and cosine increment separately in TC6. The bar is exactly
constant on the entire small slice. Otherwise take
`c_j=N_hj` for `j!=i`, and `c_i=-sum_{j!=i}N_hj`. This is a nonzero integer
affine relation, with `||c||_1<=2 sum_{j!=i}|N_hj|<=2m_h`. This proves AP1
and the affine-independence consequence. ∎

For a diameter-pair-minimal source in AP's exact-flat case, the same generic
velocity argument as PF forces every active bar to be persistent. The statement
requires positivity on **all** persistent bars: a balance on a proper subset
does not control unsupported equality bars, exactly the canonical §43.2 warning.
No globally invisible train is removed, and no motion is continued to a possible
triangle wall.

The first possible affine-relation obstruction already occurs at three levels.
For formal rotation levels `(0,x,2x)`, the vector `(1,-2,1)` has zero total and
zero first moment. Viewed from the middle level, the two moving contributions
have deviations `(-x,x)` with common orientation and give the exact positive
quartic expression `4(cos(tx)-1)^2`. This is an algebraic obstruction model, not
a realized source or a global minimizer. It shows why AP must retain the
integer-dependence case rather than declaring every potential network flat.

### 5.5. Three fully supported bars have at most one quartic coordinate

**Theorem TQ.** Suppose `J` consists of exactly three bars and `P` contains a
balance strictly positive on all three. At a local diameter minimum, either
all three bars are exactly constant on `theta(0)+K`, or, after relabeling:

- exactly one bar, `h_0`, is nonflat;
- `P` is a singleton with `mu_0<mu_-` and `mu_0<mu_+`;
- the rotation functionals satisfy `s_0=(s_-+s_+)/2`;
- for the nonzero linear functional `delta=(s_+-s_-)/2` and a nonzero integer
  signed count `a`, the exact response is

\[
\boxed{|r_0(t)|^2-D^2=4a^2(\cos(t\delta(v))-1)^2,\qquad
 |r_-(t)|=|r_+(t)|=D.}
\tag{TQ1}
\]

Thus the entire three-bar higher-order obstruction factors through **one**
relative-rotation coordinate, independently of the number of tangent trains.
The theorem is a necessary normal form, not a proof that this form is realized
by a source or that a curved correction removes it.

**Proof.** By AP, a nonflat case has three distinct rotation functionals with
affine rank one. First note that any tangent train of `h` separates precisely
one of the other two supported bars. It separates at least one by gradient
balance. If it separated both, they would both be nontangent and their rotation
functionals would equal its train-coordinate functional on `K`, contradicting
their distinctness.

If that other bar is `k`, the train coordinate of the gradient balance gives
`mu_h |(g_h)_tau|=mu_k |(g_k)_tau|`. A tangent contribution has
`|(g_h)_tau|=2D`, while the nontangent contribution of `k` has magnitude
strictly less than `2D`. Hence

\[
\boxed{h\longrightarrow k\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\mu_h<\mu_k.}
\tag{TQ2}
\]

Every nonflat bar has nonzero signed transfer counts to **both** other bars:
a sole nonzero count would contradict AP2 and distinctness of the functionals.
It therefore has smaller weight than both. There can be at most one such bar;
call it `h_0`. All other bars have zero off-diagonal signed counts and are
exactly flat. (Equivalently, a nonminimum bar has at most one possible transfer
destination, and AP2 forces that destination's signed count to vanish.)

The balance polytope is now a singleton. To see this without assuming stress
uniqueness, `h_0` has a tangent train transferring to each of the other bars.
The corresponding gradient-coordinate columns have respectively nonzero entries
on `(h_0,h_-)` and `(h_0,h_+)`, with the third entry zero. They are independent,
so the three-gradient matrix has rank at least two; the positive balance makes
its rank exactly two and its nonnegative kernel a unique ray.

CG3 therefore forces `F_0=0`, since the other two cubics vanish. Set
`u=s_--s_0` and `w=s_+-s_0`, and let the two nonzero integer signed counts be
`a,b`. Their first and third moment identities are

\[
au+bw=0,\qquad au^3+bw^3=0.
\tag{TQ3}
\]

The three rotation functionals are distinct, so `u,w,u-w` are nonzero linear
forms. Eliminating `b w=-a u` in the cubic identity gives
`a u(u-w)(u+w)=0` in the real polynomial ring on `K`. Thus `u+w=0`, and then
`a=b`. This is the midpoint identity. Substitution into TC6 cancels the sine
terms and leaves `2a(cos(t delta)-1)Je_0`, proving TQ1. ∎

The source-specific strict weight ordering TQ2 is what collapses the apparent
many-train quartic capacity here. It uses three distinct rotation classes and
does not extend to a train whose other supported incidences lie on several bars
with the **same** rotation functional: their combined weight can pay for that
train. Nor does TQ control a persistent bar omitted by every positive balance.

### 5.6. Phase-group balance eliminates the last three-bar quartic

**Theorem PG (exact phase-group balance).** Under AP's full-support hypothesis,
form a matrix `B` with one row per persistent bar and one column per distinct
rotation class. For a bar `h` in class `i`, put

\[
B_{hj}=N_{hj}\ (j\ne i),\qquad
B_{hi}=-\sum_{j\ne i}N_{hj}.
\]

For every positive gradient balance `mu` on the persistent set,

\[
\boxed{B\mathbf1=0,\qquad B s=0,\qquad \mu^{\mathsf T}B=0.}
\tag{PG1}
\]

Here `B s=0` is an identity in `K*`, and the last equation is an exact signed
flow balance, not a generic-rank inference.

**Proof.** The first two equations are AP2. For each class `j`, let `z^(j)` be
the full train-angle velocity which is one on coordinates whose restriction
to `K` equals `s_j`, and zero on all other coordinates. This is an allowed
train-angle test vector; it is **not** asserted to belong to `K`.

Every train visible to a persistent bar belongs to one of these classes:
nontangent incidences have speed `s_h`, and tangent incidences transfer by AP.
Trains invisible to all persistent bars have zero gradient components. For
`j!=i`, the total separating contribution of class `j` to bar `h` is
`N_hj J e_h`. Therefore

\[
g_h\cdot z^{(j)}=-2D N_{hj}\quad(j\ne i),\qquad
g_h\cdot z^{(i)}=2D\sum_{j\ne i}N_{hj}.
\tag{PG2}
\]

The second equality follows either by summing the contributions or from common
rotation invariance. Pairing the full gradient balance with each `z^(j)` proves
`mu^T B=0`. The test vectors need not produce a diameter-preserving motion;
only the exact first-order gradient identity is used. ∎

**Theorem TF (three-bar flattening).** If `|J|<=3` and there is a gradient
balance positive on **all** persistent bars, every persistent diameter is exactly
constant on a neighborhood of zero in `theta(0)+K`. If also `K!=0`, a
diameter-pair-minimal source in this case has no active bar outside `J`.

**Proof.** With at most two distinct rotation classes, use AP. Otherwise there
are exactly three bars with distinct rotation functionals. The strict transfer
ordering TQ2 applies. A bar that is not a strict minimum of the three weights
has at most one strictly heavier destination. AP2 then forces its off-diagonal
signed counts to be zero, because the functionals are distinct. Thus `B` has at
most one nonzero row, belonging to the unique possible strict minimum weight.
But `mu^T B=0` and all weights are positive, so that row is zero too. Every
destination class therefore cancels exactly in TC6, proving flatness. The final
diameter-pair-minimal consequence follows by choosing a generic velocity as in
PF. ∎

This proves that TQ's sole quartic normal form is **not realizable under the full
source hypotheses**: its formal response survives moment identities but violates
the phase-group gradient balance. The formal algebraic example is not rejected;
its proposed use as a fully supported three-bar source obstruction is rejected.
The direct proof of TF needs neither cubic optimization nor sign coherence.

With four or more distinct bars the strict weight ordering still holds when
all rotation functionals are distinct, and PG1 remains exact, but it no longer
forces a one-row support. When several bars share a rotation functional, even
the strict individual-weight ordering must be replaced by a combined-recipient
weight bound. These are genuine remaining network cases, not excluded here.

### 5.7. Four bars with distinct rotation functionals also flatten

**Theorem FF.** Suppose `|J|=4`, there is a gradient balance positive on every
persistent bar, and all four rotation functionals are distinct. Then every
persistent bar is exactly flat on `theta(0)+K`. Thus among fully supported
four-bar higher-order cases, the only unexcluded rotation-class multiplicity
pattern is `(2,1,1)`; one or two classes are already flat by AP.

**Proof.** Order the weights `mu_1<=mu_2<=mu_3<=mu_4`. Distinct rotation
functionals mean a tangent train of a supported bar can separate only one other
supported bar, so TQ2 still applies: a nonzero `N_ij` requires `mu_i<mu_j`.
The last two rows of `B` have at most one possible destination each and hence
vanish by `B s=0`. If the second row vanishes as well, `mu^T B=0` kills the
first row and we are done.

Otherwise `N_23` and `N_24` are both nonzero. The phase-group balance gives

\[
N_{13}=-{\mu_2\over\mu_1}N_{23},\quad
N_{14}=-{\mu_2\over\mu_1}N_{24},\quad
N_{12}={\mu_2\over\mu_1}(N_{23}+N_{24}).
\tag{FF1}
\]

The last quantity is nonzero: otherwise the second row of `B s=0` would give
`N_23(s_3-s_4)=0`, impossible. We therefore have all five transfers
`1->2`, `1->3`, `1->4`, `2->3`, and `2->4`.

This five-edge pattern is geometrically impossible. Put `v_i=mu_i e_i` for
the weighted unit diameter directions. For a tangent transfer `i->j`, its
gradient coordinate has just these two supported incidences. The exact scalar
balance therefore implies

\[
\boxed{|v_i\cdot v_j|=|v_i|^2.}
\tag{FF2}
\]

For completeness, orient the two bars so that `v_1=(a,0)` and `v_2=(a,b)`;
this is possible by `1->2`, and `a>0`, `b!=0` since distinct diameter bars
are not parallel. A common higher recipient `v_k` of both can be oriented
as `(a,y)`. Its second projection equation is

\[
|a^2+by|=a^2+b^2.
\]

The only possibilities are `y=b` and `y=-b-2a^2/b`. The former equals `v_2`
and cannot be a strictly higher recipient. The latter gives only **one**
unoriented diameter direction. There cannot be two distinct bars `3,4` with
that direction. This contradicts the five transfers, so `B=0`. TC6 now proves
exact flatness. ∎

FF uses the actual two-dimensional diameter directions, in addition to the
integer phase matrix. Neither a formal signed-flow example nor an arbitrary
upper-triangular matrix can supply the prohibited five-edge source pattern.
The `(2,1,1)` case has multiple possible recipients in its double class and
does not satisfy the two-incidence argument; it remains open here.

**Arithmetic continuation.** The separate
[five-bar candidate](target_b_five_bar_arithmetic_flattening_2026-09-22.md)
extends distinct-functional flattening through five bars. It exhausts the 1024
forward transfer supports and excludes the six remaining patterns using exact
integer-count rationality and two certified rank-zero elliptic curves. Unlike
the present elementary proofs, that continuation imports explicitly sourced
standard rational 2-isogeny descent and good-reduction torsion injection.

## 6. Sharpness and an odd-support regression

The paired spaces in TC2 attain the capacity for even support. For odd support,
append one zero coordinate to obtain `floor(m/2)` as well. However, maximal
odd-support spaces need not be paired, even if every coordinate is active.

Put `r=sqrt(5)` and define five linear forms `w_i=a_i x+b_i y` by

\[
a=(1,(r-1)/4,-(r+1)/4,-(r+1)/4,(r-1)/4),
\qquad b=(0,1,(r-1)/2,-(r-1)/2,-1).
\]

Exactly,

\[
\sum_iw_i=\sum_iw_i^3=0,
\qquad \sum_iw_i^2={5\over2}x^2+(5-r)y^2>0\quad((x,y)\ne0).
\tag{TC13}
\]

Every pair of coefficient vectors `(a_i,b_i)` is nonproportional. This is a
rank-two cubic-flat space with five active coordinates, attaining `floor(5/2)`
without a paired coordinate representation. It rejects an extension of TC2
to odd support. It is an algebraic tangent model, not a geometric packing or
a global minimizer.

Mixed signs must also be retained. Even when signed cubic moments vanish,
the signed second moment can be indefinite or zero. TC8 remains valid; the
strict quartic conclusion TC10 requires common orientation. No universal
geometric sign-coherence theorem is imported from historical reports.

There is a second exact regression against replacing *individual-bar*
nondecrease in TB by nondecrease of the maximum over bars. Use formal tangent
vectors `w=(x,y,-x-y)` for one bar and `-w` on a separate three-coordinate
support for the other. The two image ranks are two, exceeding `floor(3/2)`,
and their cubic forms are `-3xy(x+y)` and `+3xy(x+y)`. Write
`A=sum sin(tw_i)` and `B=sum(cos(tw_i)-1)`. The maximum of the two squared
bar changes is exactly

\[
\max\{(D-A)^2+B^2-D^2,(D+A)^2+B^2-D^2\}
=2D|A|+A^2+B^2\ge0.
\tag{TC14}
\]

Thus a cubic cusp can block every direction without either individual cubic
vanishing. The two cubics share factors, exactly as the two-bar consequence
requires. TC14 is an algebraic model, not a realization by diameter chords
in a packing. It prevents an invalid promotion from TC8 to a universal
local-minimum rank bound.

## 7. Verification, classification, and residual obligation

Run

```text
python3 research/verify_target_b_tangent_cubic_capacity_2026_09_21.py
python3 tools/check_theorem_state.py
git diff --check
```

The verifier checks the arbitrary-sign expansion through order six, paired
sharpness for dimensions one through six, TC13 over `Q(sqrt(5))`, and finite
integer graph charts for the even-support classification. It exhausts 625
two-dimensional charts and 19683 three-dimensional charts, obtaining respectively
2 and 6 survivors, all paired. It also verifies JC3 through four paired
coordinates, a semidefinite-cofactor example at even-support capacity, and every
two-bar assignment of up to eight paired coordinates. These bounded checks are independent sanity
checks, not a substitute for TC's proof over all real subspaces. The curved
zero-gradient descent, exact flatness identities, cubic corrector, three-level
regression, and TQ midpoint elimination / strict weight ordering are also checked.
SymPy exact
arithmetic is used; neither floating-point evidence nor a global-optimality
certificate is involved.

All new theorem claims TC, TB, TC11–TC12, CD, JC, CG, PF, AP, TQ, PG, TF, and FF are **proved candidates
awaiting promotion**. TC13 is an exact **regression** against odd-support
pairing rigidity, and TC14 is a **regression** against applying an individual-bar
capacity to a joint cubic cusp. The generated report header remains **unreviewed**, consistent
with the absence of an independent promotion audit. No canonical state or prompt
is changed.

The three-level model in §5.4 is a **regression** against unconditional flattening
of dependent rotation potentials. It checks the response shape later forced by
TQ, but does not establish that any source realizes that shape. Selection of a
diameter-pair-minimal source in PF/AP is conditional on that selected source
having the stated pure-disk contact complex; no purity-preserving selection
theorem is claimed.

The square and canonical paired quartic example are retained by the sharpness
cases. Regular-shell regressions lack the pure-rhombic train-coordinate
hypotheses. The five-sector and vanishing-shear examples are not excluded merely
by density, positive stress, or small angular defect. All statements concern the
same source throughout; no source points are deleted. The finite descent in CD
preserves every contact simultaneously and handles every inactive pair by its
actual positive slack.

The joint theorem JC bounds abstract correlated two-bar obstructions by half
their total active support, with parity improvements. The stronger source-specific
theorem PF then shows that at a local minimum with `D>1`, two persistent bars
cannot actually supply any cubic or quartic obstruction: both are exactly flat.
Its new ingredient is the zero-gradient descent plus the all-equality-bar
specialization of the canonical tangent transfer; the transfer itself and the
secondary-cusp separation step were already canonical.

The new remaining algebraic obstruction is precise: on the actual backward-kernel
space, persistent equality bars must either have zero cubics subject to TC's sharp
rank budget, or have cubic forms with no common strict negative direction. With
two persistent bars and `D>1`, PF eliminates all higher-order blocking and leaves
an exact same-diameter family. For three or more bars, CG reduces cubic survival
to the actual persistent-gradient stress polytope, but does not close it. With
full support on the persistent bars, AP further requires a bounded integer affine
relation among the actual rotation functionals for any nonflat bar. TF eliminates
the entire fully supported three-bar higher-order case: the intermediate midpoint
quartic fails PG's exact source-gradient balance. The remaining higher-order
network cases therefore require at least four persistent bars, or failure of
full support on the three-bar set. FF further excludes four distinct rotation
classes on four bars; the four-bar pattern `(2,1,1)` is not excluded. Target B
still requires excluding the surviving
same-diameter flat families and multi-bar
obstructions together with the full backward-rank case, and then treating the
topology corrections. No claim
closes Target A, Target B, C-II, or Level I; C-I stays closed.